        self.left = None
        self.right = None

def isSameTree(p: TreeNode, q: TreeNode) -> bool:
    def check(p, q):
        if not p and not q:
            return True
        if not p or not q:
            return False
        if p.val != q.val:
            return False

        left_height_p, right_height_p = 0, 0
        if p.left:
            left_height_p = maxHeight(p.left) + 1
        if p.right:
            right_height_p = maxHeight(p.right) + 1

        left_height_q, right_height_q = 0, 0
        if q.left:
            left_height_q = maxHeight(q.left) + 1
        if q.right:
            right_height_q = maxHeight(q.right) + 1

        if left_height_p != left_height_q or right_height_p != right_height_q:
            return False

        return check(p.left, q.left) and check(p.right, q.right)

    def maxHeight(node):
        if node is None:
            return 0
        return max(maxHeight(node.left), maxHeight(node.right)) + 1

    return check(p, q)
```

This script also introduces a class TreeNode to define a binary tree. `isSameTree(p: TreeNode, q: TreeNode)` is a function which takes two binary trees and checks if they are same or not. It uses a helper function `check(p, q)`. The `maxHeight(node)` function calculates the maximum depth/height of a binary tree.

Building out an entire Lovecraftian puzzle game is a complex task that would typically take a team of developers and designers considerably longer than a simple Q&A response can encompass. However, I can guide you through the creation of a small part of a puzzle game in C# for Unity that could fit into your Lovecraftian setting.

This example will set the stage for a puzzle where the player must find and arrange the books you mentioned in a specific order to unlock a hidden compartment in a bookshelf, revealing a vital clue or key item to progress in the game.

1. Setting up the scene in Unity:
- Create a new Unity project and set up a scene to represent the room with the bookshelf.
- Import suitable bookshelf and book models, or you can create simple placeholders if you don't have ready models.
- Lay out the books on the bookshelf or around the room.

2. Creating the book script in C#:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class Book : MonoBehaviour
{
    // Book identification
    public string bookTitle;

    // Positioning for the correct placement of the book
    public Transform correctSlot;

    private bool isPlacedCorrectly = false;

    // Check if the book is placed correctly, allowing for some leeway in positioning
    private void Update()
    {
        if (Vector3.Distance(transform.position, correctSlot.position) < 0.1f)
        {
            if (!isPlacedCorrectly)
            {
                isPlacedCorrectly = true;
                BookshelfManager.Instance.CheckPuzzleCompletion();
            }
        }
        else
        {
            if (isPlacedCorrectly)
            {
                isPlacedCorrectly = false;
                BookshelfManager.Instance.CheckPuzzleCompletion();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This script will be attached to each book. It has a public string to identify the book and a `Transform` to represent the correct slot where the book must be placed to be part of the successfully completed puzzle.

3. Creating the BookshelfManager script in C#:

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class BookshelfManager : MonoBehaviour
{
    public static BookshelfManager Instance;

    // These are the correct titles in order from left to right
    private readonly string[] solutionTitles = new string[] {
        "Invocation of the Cosmic Horror",
        "The House of R'lyeh",
        "Mark of the Old Gods",
        "The Shadow People",
        "The Unknown and the Deep Void"
    };

    // Books that the player can interact with on the bookshelf
    public Book[] booksInShelf;

    private void Awake()
    {
        // Singleton pattern
        if (Instance == null)
            Instance = this;
        else
            Destroy(gameObject);
    }

    public void CheckPuzzleCompletion()
    {
        // Check if all books are placed correctly
        for (int i = 0; i < booksInShelf.Length; i++)
        {
            if (booksInShelf[i].bookTitle != solutionTitles[i])
                return; // If any book is incorrect return immediately
        }

        // All books are correctly placed
        OnPuzzleSolved();
    }

    private void OnPuzzleSolved()
    {
        Debug.Log("Puzzle Solved! Secret compartment opens.");
        // Here you can implement the logic for the secret compartment opening
    }
}
```

Attach the `BookshelfManager` script to the bookshelf game object. Then, drag your book game objects into the `booksInShelf` array in the order in which they must be arranged to solve the puzzle. Make sure to input the correct titles in the `solutionTitles` array.

4. Adding interaction:

Typically, you’d have a player controller that can move and look around. The books should have colliders, and when the player interacts (a simple button press), you could enable a system to pick up the book. Dragging and dropping the books using the mouse or a controller can be done by changing their position based on where the player is pointing.

Please note that implementing the full interaction logic and the mechanics to allow the player to pick up, carry, and place the books correctly requires additional input management, physics interactions, and game logic that Unity supports but isn't fleshed out here due to the complexity. Resources, like the Unity Manual and Scripting API, as well as community forums, are very useful for digging deeper into those topics.

By following the above steps, expanding, and refining the code, you can build a Lovecraftian puzzle that would contribute to the overall atmosphere and narrative of your Unity game.

String interning is a method of storing only one copy of each distinct string value, which must be immutable. The string interning pool is used to reduce the memory usage when you have many instances of the same string. 

In the .NET Framework, string interning isn't automatically applied when you concatenate strings or build them using StringBuilder. When you concatenate strings or build them using StringBuilder, a new string object is created in memory to hold the resulting string. This new string object is not in the intern pool, unless you explicitly add it with the String.Intern method. 

So in simple words, string concatenation or using StringBuilder doesn't change anything for the interned strings, they don't put the new string into intern pool either, unless explicitly asked to do so.

For instance:

string s = new StringBuilder().Append("Hello").Append(" ").Append("World").ToString();

// s is not added in the string intern pool

string poolString = string.Intern(s);

// Now s is added in the string intern pool

When you call String.Intern, it checks whether the string you are passing as an argument is already in the intern pool. If it's there, it returns a reference to that string. If it's not there, it adds the string to the pool and then returns a reference to it. This explicit call to intern is rare, but could be used in scenarios where you know you'll be using the same string repetitively and you want to save memory.

Remember that interning strings can be costly (in terms of time) so it should be done when the benefits clearly outweight the costs. In many cases, relying on the .NET runtime to manage memory for you (without interning strings) is sufficient and leads to cleaner, simpler code.

Here is an example of how to create a Sass Mixin for a "toast" style notification with customizable background and text color, position and transparency.

First, we will define a Mixin called "toastStyle" which accepts parameters for background color and text color:

```sass
// Define a mixin for toast style
@mixin toastStyle($bgColor, $txtColor, $position, $transparency) {
  background-color: $bgColor;
  color: $txtColor;
  border-radius: 5px;
  padding: 10px;
  position: absolute;
  // Default values for opacity and position
  opacity: 1;
  top: initial;
  bottom: initial;
  left: initial;
  right: initial;
  // Set position
  @if $position == 'top' {
    top: 0;
  } @else if $position == 'bottom' {
    bottom: 0;
  } @else if $position == 'left' {
    left: 0;
  } @else if $position == 'right' {
    right: 0;
  } @else if $position == 'center' {
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  }
  // Set transparency
  opacity: $transparency;
}
```

Next, you would use this mixin to style a class, ID, or HTML element like so:

```sass
// Use mixin for .toast class with blue background, white text, placed at the center, and 75% opaque
.toast {
  @include toastStyle(blue, white, 'center', 0.75);
}
```

In this example, the toast notifications will be centered on the screen with a blue background and white text, and will be 75% opaque. You can change the parameters to customize the appearance and position as needed.

